<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Airlock as a macOS LaunchDaemon.

  Install:

    sudo cp deploy/com.airlock.server.plist /Library/LaunchDaemons/
    sudo chown root:wheel /Library/LaunchDaemons/com.airlock.server.plist
    sudo chmod 600 /Library/LaunchDaemons/com.airlock.server.plist
    sudo mkdir -p /usr/local/var/airlock
    sudo chmod 700 /usr/local/var/airlock
    sudo launchctl bootstrap system /Library/LaunchDaemons/com.airlock.server.plist

  Remove:

    sudo launchctl bootout system/com.airlock.server

  Logs:

    tail -f /var/log/airlock.log /var/log/airlock.err.log

  Airlock never forks. launchd supervises the process it starts, so a program
  that daemonizes and exits is a program launchd believes has died, and
  KeepAlive then restarts a corpse in a loop. The binary stays in the
  foreground and that is the whole arrangement.

  The data directory is given as an absolute path because launchd hands a
  daemon no useful working directory. WorkingDirectory is set as well, so
  neither answer depends on the other. A relative path here would create a
  fresh salt and an empty store somewhere unexpected, which reads as data loss
  rather than as a path mistake.

  UNVERIFIED, and the first thing to check on a Mac: whether the App Store and
  Standalone builds of Tailscale serve the certificate endpoint at all. If they
  do not, host mode has no TLS on this machine, therefore no secure context,
  therefore no install, push, share target or download. Nothing here was run on
  macOS. The check is one command, run as the user Airlock will run as:

    tailscale cert your-node.your-tailnet.ts.net

  If that is refused, use the Tailscale package that does serve it, or run
  Airlock in embedded mode, where it carries its own node and its own
  certificate and needs no daemon.

  UNVERIFIED, and the second thing to check: whether binding one specific
  address on port 443 still requires root on macOS. Mojave lifted the reserved
  port restriction for wildcard binds, and Airlock binds the machine's own
  tailnet addresses on purpose so the listener is never reachable from the LAN.
  Do not switch to a wildcard bind to dodge this, because that throws away the
  property the specific bind exists to give. A LaunchDaemon runs as root, which
  makes the question moot for this file; it becomes live the moment you add a
  UserName below. Any port above 1024, passed with the port flag, retires it
  entirely.

  App Store Tailscale is the case this file does not cover. That build
  discovers its API token per GUI user and root cannot see it, so a daemon
  cannot reach the local API at all. Use the same file as a LaunchAgent
  instead: put it in ~/Library/LaunchAgents, point the data path at somewhere
  the user owns, and load it with

    launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.airlock.server.plist

  An agent runs only while that user is logged in, which is the price.

  ponytail: this daemon runs as root, which is the default for anything in
  /Library/LaunchDaemons and is what keeps certificate fetching working with no
  extra grant. The ceiling is that the whole server has root on the box rather
  than only what it needs. Upgrade by creating a dedicated user, adding
  UserName and GroupName keys here, chowning the data directory to it, and
  granting that user the local API with `sudo tailscale set` and the operator
  flag. Verify certificates still issue afterwards, since that is the call that
  needs more than read.

  (XML comments cannot contain two hyphens in a row, which is why flags are
  named in prose here rather than written out. They appear verbatim in
  ProgramArguments below, and in full in the README.)
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>com.airlock.server</string>

	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<string>/usr/local/bin/airlock</string>
		<string>--data</string>
		<string>/usr/local/var/airlock</string>
		<string>--port</string>
		<string>443</string>
	</array>

	<key>WorkingDirectory</key>
	<string>/usr/local/var/airlock</string>

	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<true/>

	<key>KeepAlive</key>
	<true/>

	<key>StandardOutPath</key>
	<string>/var/log/airlock.log</string>

	<key>StandardErrorPath</key>
	<string>/var/log/airlock.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
